 24: 
 25: const SHADOW_SX = 2000 / TILE_CM;
 26: const SHADOW_SY = 4000 / TILE_CM;
 27: 
 28: const GROUND_OFFSET = 5 / TILE_CM;
 29: 
 30: /**
 31:  * How dark a shadowed pixel lands. `RenderBodyShadow` uses
 32:  * `glColor4f(0, 0, 0, 0.5)`; this sits above it because the original draws
 33:  * onto a terrain that is already dimmed by its own vertex lighting, and this
 34:  * one draws onto a lit, tone-mapped floor where a flat half-black reads as a
 35:  * grey smudge - most visibly on snow, where the ground is the brightest thing
 36:  * on screen. The stencil below still means overlaps never deepen it, so this
 37:  * is the shadow's one and only value.
 38:  *
 39:  * The Classic value of the one shadow rule (lighting_polish ARCHITECTURE
 40:  * §4.4): the blob keeps this alpha whatever the policy says, because Classic
 41:  * is the reference and does not move.
 42:  */
 43: const SHADOW_ALPHA = 0.65;
 44: 
 45: /**
 46:  * `EnableAlphaTest` - glAlphaFunc(GL_GREATER, 0.25), ZzzOpenglUtil.cpp:395.
 47:  * The silhouette is cut by the caster's own texture at the same threshold the
 48:  * caster is drawn with, so a mesh that is a card with a shape keyed into it
 49:  * (a sword's guard, a wing membrane, a leaf spray) throws that shape and not
 50:  * its quad.
 51:  *
 52:  * The original does not do this - `RenderBodyShadow` calls `DisableTexture()`
 53:  * and every triangle lands solid. It gets away with it because it only ever
 54:  * shadows body parts and weapons, whose meshes *are* their silhouette; the
 55:  * blobs here also cover map objects, where an untextured card reads as a
 56:  * black rectangle on the floor.
 57:  *
 58:  * Well below the 0.25 the caster itself is drawn with, though, and
 59:  * deliberately: the caster is keyed to hide a card's corners, the shadow is
 60:  * keyed only to find the silhouette's outline. MU's TGAs carry partial alpha
 61:  * across whole surfaces and not just along a key's edge - a cape's weave, a
 62:  * skirt's hem, a wing membrane, a helmet's visor - and at 0.25 every one of
 63:  * those surfaces fell under the test and punched a hole clean through the
 64:  * middle of the shadow, along the seam of whichever body part wore it. At 0.1
 65:  * only a texel the artist actually keyed out is cut, which is the outline and
 66:  * nothing else, and the interior lands solid the way the original's does.
 67:  */
 68: const SHADOW_ALPHA_CUTOFF = 0.1;
 69: 
 70: /**
 71:  * ### Why there is only one slot, and only the sun direction
 72:  *
 73:  * There was a second projection here: the silhouette a torch throws across the
 74:  * floor *away from the flame*, on its own stencil bit, with the blob under a
 75:  * character warped between the two. It was removed on request - outdoors it
 76:  * put a second shadow under everything within reach of a brazier, and indoors
 77:  * it exposed three separate faults at once: the room's own walls and roof cast
 78:  * across the floor they enclose (the Lorencia pub's roof is lifted 100 units
 79:  * out of view while the hero is inside, and went on casting from up there),
 80:  * every long weapon smeared into a streak because the projection stretches a
 81:  * caster by its height, and the furniture that should have cast did not,
 82:  * because `ModelObject.BLOB_SHADOW_MIN_HEIGHT` is measured against the tallest
 83:  * single mesh, and a table is a thin top on thin legs.
 84:  *
 85:  * Each of those is fixable. Together they are a feature that needs a real
 86:  * shadow map rather than a projection, and the cascades already are one. So:
 87:  * one slot, the sun direction, and the CSM replaces it wherever it runs.
 88:  */
 89: const SHADOW_STENCIL_BITS = [0x80] as const;
 90: 
 91: export const SHADOW_SLOTS = SHADOW_STENCIL_BITS.length;
 92: 
 93: const SHADOW_FADE_START = 250 / TILE_CM;
 94: const SHADOW_FADE_END = 600 / TILE_CM;
 95: 
 96: /**
 97:  * How far the silhouette is pushed out along its own surface normal, in MU
 98:  * units, before it is projected.
 99:  *
100:  * A MU body is not one skin. It is a stack of separate BMDs - body, helm,
101:  * armour, pants, gloves, boots, plus whatever is in each hand - and inside
102:  * each of those every vertex is bound rigidly to exactly one bone
103:  * (`bmdToGlb`: `weightsArray.push(1, 0, 0, 0)`). Nothing blends across a
104:  * joint, so wherever two pieces meet they simply overlap, and the overlap is
105:  * only as deep as the artist left it. Seen in 3D that is invisible - one
106:  * piece is in front of the other. Flattened onto the floor it is not: the
107:  * two silhouettes are laid side by side in the same plane, and every place
108:  * their overlap ran out shows as a bright hairline of unshadowed ground -
109:  * around the neck, at each elbow and knee, where a boot meets a shin. A
110:  * shadow that ought to be one shape arrives as a pile of parts.
111:  *
112:  * Growing each piece by a hair along its own normal makes the overlaps deep
113:  * enough to survive being flattened, and the seams close. Only the horizontal
114:  * part of the normal is used, because the vertical part cannot change a
115:  * silhouette that has already been squashed flat - see the vertex block.
116:  *
117:  * Kept small: this is the whole outline growing, not just the joints, so it
118:  * is also what the shadow's edge costs in accuracy. Three units is under a
119:  * tenth of a limb's width and roughly what the joints were short by.
120:  */
121: const SHADOW_DILATE = 3 / TILE_CM;
122: 
123: /**
124:  * How far above the sampled ground the flattened shadow sits, in world units.
125:  * The vertex block reads the height from the bilinear heightmap while the
126:  * terrain mesh is two triangles per tile, so on any slope the two disagree by
127:  * a hair and the shadow would z-fight or dip under the floor. Two units is
128:  * more than that disagreement on walkable ground and less than a boot sole,
129:  * so the caster's own feet still win the depth test against it.
130:  */
131: const SHADOW_LIFT = 2 / TILE_CM;
132: 
133: const shadowMaterials: (CustomMaterial | null)[] = [];
134: 
135: function createShadowMaterial(scene: Scene, slot: number): CustomMaterial {
136:   const material = new CustomMaterial(`objectShadow${slot}`, scene);
137: 
138:   material.diffuseColor = Color3.Black();
139:   material.specularColor = Color3.Black();
140:   material.emissiveColor = Color3.Black();
141:   material.ambientColor = Color3.Black();
142:   material.disableLighting = true;
143: 
144:   // Deliberately 1 rather than SHADOW_ALPHA. This value never reaches the
145:   // blend - the shadow's opacity is assigned in the fragment below - but it
146:   // does reach the alpha *test*, because Babylon folds it in before the
147:   // compare: `alpha = vDiffuseColor.a * texel.a`, then `alpha < alphaCutOff`
148:   // (ALPHATEST_AFTERALLALPHACOMPUTATIONS, which any explicit
149:   // `transparencyMode` turns on - default.fragment:246). At 0.5 the test read
150:   // `texel.a < 0.5`, five times the threshold below, and every soft key went
151:   // with it: a feathered wing edge, hair, a fringe, a blade's ground bevel.
152:   material.alpha = 1;
153: 
154:   // Alpha-keyed casters cut their own silhouette (see SHADOW_ALPHA_CUTOFF).
155:   // The texture is a per-mesh bind in `onBindObservable` below, exactly as on
156:   // the shared item material; this one is the placeholder that compiles the
157:   // DIFFUSE / ALPHATEST / ALPHAFROMDIFFUSE defines in. `diffuseColor` is
158:   // black, so the texel only ever reaches the alpha.
159:   material.diffuseTexture = getEmptyTexture(scene);
160:   material.useAlphaFromDiffuseTexture = true;
161:   material.transparencyMode = Material.MATERIAL_ALPHATESTANDBLEND;
162:   material.alphaCutOff = SHADOW_ALPHA_CUTOFF;
163:   material.backFaceCulling = false;
164: 
165:   material.disableDepthWrite = true;
166: 
167:   // A constant depth bias only - enough to beat the coplanar ground it was
168:   // just draped on (with SHADOW_LIFT), never the caster. The slope-scaled
169:   // `zOffset = -2` that stood here pulled a flattened polygon seen at the
170:   // camera's grazing angle far enough forward to beat the character's legs:
171:   // on Classic the weapon's shadow was drawn *through* the character. CSM
172:   // never had the problem, and now neither does this.
173:   material.zOffset = 0;
174:   material.zOffsetUnits = -2;
175: 
176:   const bit = SHADOW_STENCIL_BITS[slot];
177: 
178:   material.stencil.enabled = true;
179:   material.stencil.func = Constants.NOTEQUAL;
180:   material.stencil.funcRef = bit;
181:   material.stencil.funcMask = bit;
182:   material.stencil.opStencilFail = Constants.KEEP;
183: 
184:   material.stencil.opDepthFail = Constants.KEEP;
185: 
186:   material.stencil.opStencilDepthPass = Constants.REPLACE;
187: 
188:   material.stencil.mask = bit;
189: 
190:   material.AddUniform('shadowOrigin', 'vec3', undefined);
191:   material.AddUniform('shadowParams', 'vec4', undefined);
192:   // The lean axis, from the key rig's sun (lighting_rework.md §5.2): the
193:   // unit horizontal direction the projection folds the caster along. (1, 0)
194:   // reproduces the old hardcoded +X formula exactly.
195:   material.AddUniform('shadowSunDir', 'vec2', undefined);
196:   material.AddUniform('terrainHeightMap', 'sampler2D', undefined);
197: 
198:   // The silhouette dilation (SHADOW_DILATE) needs the caster's normals, and
199:   // nothing else in this pass does - `disableLighting` is on, there is no
200:   // bump map and no reflection, so `_needNormals` is false and Babylon leaves
201:   // the NORMAL define, the attribute and `vNormalW` out of the compiled
202:   // shader (`PrepareDefinesForAttributes`, materialHelper.functions:708).
203:   // Ask for the buffer explicitly and declare the attribute ourselves, under
204:   // the guard that keeps this from colliding with Babylon's own declaration
205:   // if some later change ever does turn NORMAL on.
206:   material.AddAttribute('normal');
207: 
208:   material.Vertex_Definitions(`
209:     #ifndef NORMAL
210:       attribute vec3 normal;
211:     #endif
212: 
213:     varying float vShadowHeight;
214:   `);
215: 
216:   material.Vertex_After_WorldPosComputed(`
217:     // Grow the caster before flattening it - see SHADOW_DILATE. finalWorld
218:     // already carries this vertex's bone (Babylon's bonesVertex include folds
219:     // the skinning matrix into it a few lines above), so this is the normal
220:     // in the same posed world space worldPos is in.
221:     //
222:     // Only the horizontal component is kept. The projection below overwrites
223:     // worldPos.y with the terrain height, so anything pushed along the
224:     // vertical part of the normal is thrown away a moment later; on a
225:     // top-facing polygon that leaves no dilation at all, which is right -
226:     // those are the interior of the silhouette, not its outline. Faces that
227:     // do bound the outline, and the ring of faces around the open end of
228:     // every limb segment, all point sideways and get the full push.
229:     vec3 shadowNormalW = mat3(finalWorld) * normal;
230:     vec2 shadowNormalXZ = vec2(shadowNormalW.x, shadowNormalW.z);
231:     float shadowNormalLen = length(shadowNormalXZ);
232: 
233:     if (shadowNormalLen > 0.0001) {
234:       // A mesh with no normals binds this attribute to (0,0,0) rather than
235:       // failing, so the guard doubles as the fallback: no normals, no
236:       // dilation, and the shadow is what it was.
237:       worldPos.xz +=
238:         (shadowNormalXZ / shadowNormalLen) * ${SHADOW_DILATE.toFixed(5)};
239:     }
240: 
241:     #ifdef INSTANCES
242:       // A prop batch (common/propBatches.ts): the caster is one thin
243:       // instance, and its origin is the instance matrix's translation - the
244:       // loader's basis change carries none, so this is the record's position.
245:       vec3 shadowOriginI = (world * mat4(world0, world1, world2, world3))[3].xyz;
246:     #else
247:       vec3 shadowOriginI = shadowOrigin.xyz;
248:     #endif
249: 
250:     vec3 shadowRel = worldPos.xyz - shadowOriginI;
251: 
252:     float shadowDenom = min(shadowRel.y - shadowParams.y, -0.001);
253: 
254:     float shadowCasterY = worldPos.y;
255: 
256:     // The original's projection folds the caster along a single horizontal
257:     // axis (RenderBodyShadow hardcodes +X). Rotate into the sun frame
258:     // instead: shadowA runs along the lean axis and takes the original's
259:     // perspective fold, shadowB runs across it and passes through. With
260:     // shadowSunDir = (1, 0) this is bit-for-bit the old formula, and with
261:     // the rig's sun every tier's blobs lean the same way the CSM projects.
262:     vec2 shadowAcross = vec2(-shadowSunDir.y, shadowSunDir.x);
263:     float shadowA = dot(shadowRel.xz, shadowSunDir);
264:     float shadowB = dot(shadowRel.xz, shadowAcross);
265: 
266:     shadowA += (shadowRel.y * (shadowA + shadowParams.x)) / shadowDenom;
267: 
268:     worldPos.x = shadowOriginI.x
269:       + shadowSunDir.x * shadowA + shadowAcross.x * shadowB;
270:     worldPos.z = shadowOriginI.z
271:       + shadowSunDir.y * shadowA + shadowAcross.y * shadowB;
272: 
273:     vec2 shadowUV = (vec2(worldPos.x, worldPos.z) + 0.5) / ${TERRAIN_SIZE}.0;
274: 
275:     float shadowGroundY =
276:       texture2D(terrainHeightMap, shadowUV).r * shadowParams.w
277:       + shadowParams.z;
278: 
279:     worldPos.y = shadowGroundY + ${SHADOW_LIFT.toFixed(5)};
280: 
281:     vShadowHeight = shadowCasterY - shadowGroundY;
282:   `);
283: 
284:   material.Fragment_Definitions('varying float vShadowHeight;');
285: 
286:   material.Fragment_Before_FragColor(`
287:     // Flat, from SHADOW_ALPHA, the way glColor4f(0, 0, 0, 0.5) is flat in
288:     // RenderBodyShadow - and not the
289:     // texel's alpha, which Babylon has already multiplied in by this point
290:     // (ALPHAFROMDIFFUSE, default.fragment:114). The texture is in this pass to
291:     // cut the key and for nothing else. MU's TGAs carry partial alpha across
292:     // whole surfaces and not only along a key's edge - a cape's weave, a wing
293:     // membrane, a lantern's glass - so letting it through faded each shadow in
294:     // proportion to how see-through its caster was, and a shadow that is a
295:     // little bit of everything is the one that reads as too thin. The original
296:     // samples no texture here at all: every triangle it keeps lands solid 50%
297:     // black, and overlaps do not deepen it (the stencil above).
298:     color.a = ${SHADOW_ALPHA.toFixed(3)};
299: 
300:     color.a *= 1.0 - smoothstep(
301:       ${SHADOW_FADE_START.toFixed(4)},
302:       ${SHADOW_FADE_END.toFixed(4)},
303:       vShadowHeight
304:     );
305: 
306:     if (color.a < 0.004) discard;
307:   `);
308: 
309:   material.onBindObservable.add(mesh => {
310:     const effect = material.getEffect();
311:     if (!effect) return;
312: 
313:     // The caster's own texture, carried onto the clone by `createOneShadow`.
314:     // Only its alpha is read (the material's diffuse colour is black), and
315:     // only to cut the key - see SHADOW_ALPHA_CUTOFF.
316:     effect.setTexture(
317:       'diffuseSampler',
318:       (mesh.metadata?.diffuseTexture as Texture | undefined) ??
319:         getEmptyTexture(mesh.getScene())
320:     );
321: 
322:     const heightMap = getTerrainHeightMap();
323:     if (!heightMap) return;
324: 
