 56: /**
 57:  * How the original places weapons (RenderLinkObject, ZzzCharacter.cpp:6439-6760):
 58:  *
 59:  *  - In the hands: called with Link=false (:9961) - the weapon frame *is* the
 60:  *    hand bone frame, no matrix at all.
 61:  *  - On the back (RenderCharacterBackItem :14953, bone 47): Link=true with an
 62:  *    explicit matrix in BMD bone space - AngleMatrix(70,0,90) and a per-type
 63:  *    offset (weapons (-20,5,40), bows (-10,5,10), shields (-10,0,0), cm) -
 64:  *    and for left-hand items an extra AngleMatrix(145,0,275)+(0,10,-30)
 65:  *    concatenated on the inside (:6740-6752).
 66:  *
 67:  * The GLB keeps every bone node in BMD model space (the loader's basis change
 68:  * only touches the model root), so the link matrix is used as-is in bone
 69:  * space; ModelObject.Update undoes the item root's own basis change
 70:  * underneath it. The 3x4 maths below is a literal port of ZzzMathLib.cpp,
 71:  * *including* the fact that the left-item flip is concatenated in place
 72:  * (`R_ConcatTransforms(Matrix, mNewRot, Matrix)`): `out` aliases `in1`, so
 73:  * later elements read already-overwritten ones (see boneLink.ts). The result
 74:  * is a sheared,
 75:  * non-orthogonal matrix ~64 cm away from the clean product - and that is what
 76:  * the original renders and what its constants were tuned against. A clean
 77:  * multiply puts the secondary item a tile away from the character.
 78:  *
 79:  * Debug overrides (dev only, URL query): `?backRot=70,0,90` (deg, BMD space),
 80:  * `?backOff=-20,5,40` (cm, BMD space).
 81:  */
 82: const BACK_WEAPON: BmdLink = { angle: [70, 0, 90], offset: [-20, 5, 40] };
